  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a bosonic lattice system coupled to engineered dissipation that selectively stabilizes pairs of particles. On the left, a cloud of many mobile particles is shown, with only a few highlighted as close red pairs inside dashed ovals. Wavy arrows labeled with a decay rate suggest emission or loss processes, while small blue arrows indicate motion or scattering among particles. The overall message is that, within a largely dilute background, a special mechanism creates or preserves bound two-particle states.\n\nThe right side makes this idea more concrete using a one-dimensional Bose–Hubbard chain. Particles occupy sites of a periodic lattice and can tunnel between neighboring wells, indicated by the hopping label \\(J\\). When two particles meet on the same site, the on-site interaction \\(U\\) becomes relevant. This chain is connected to a “dissipative stabilizer,” drawn as auxiliary elements labeled \\(Q_1\\) and \\(R\\), with one channel driven coherently and another providing dissipation. Arrows linking the chain to this stabilizer imply controlled exchange between the lattice and the auxiliary system. Taken together, the schematic suggests an open quantum setup in which coherent driving plus tailored loss is used to prepare or maintain interacting boson pairs in the lattice.",
